Reject a gradient value that holds a partial stop

The value of customgradient<N> holds the border and then one pair of
numbers for each stop. The value holds one triple for each stop when the
border name carries the alpha suffix. The reader tests the length with a
modulo and stores the remainder in a boolean. Every length that the
stride does not divide then passes the test.

An alpha value of five, eight or eleven elements therefore reaches the
parse loop. The loop reads one whole stop for each turn and tests no
end. The loop then steps past the last element and reads the memory
after the list. A build with the address sanitizer faults in that read.

Test the length against the exact form of the two layouts. A value that
leaves a part of a stop now takes the path of a value that does not
parse. The reader drops the gradient.

A legal value keeps its result. An alpha value of seven, ten or thirteen
elements and a plain value of any odd length parse as before.

The change sits on the branch work/config-io of the local tree, as commit 16 of 18.
A new merge request on system/qtcurve takes that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

diff --git a/qt6/common/config_file.cpp b/qt6/common/config_file.cpp
index d0d8ce01..f97bad21 100644
--- a/qt6/common/config_file.cpp
+++ b/qt6/common/config_file.cpp
@@ -1574,7 +1574,9 @@ bool qtcReadConfig(const QString &file, Options *opts, Options *defOpts, bool ch
                     Gradient                   grad;
 
                     grad.border=toGradientBorder(TO_LATIN1((*it)), &haveAlpha);
-                    ok=vals.size()%(haveAlpha ? 3 : 2);
+                    // The border takes one element and each stop takes two
+                    // elements, or three elements with an alpha.
+                    ok=1==vals.size()%(haveAlpha ? 3 : 2);
 
                     for(++it; it!=end && ok; ++it)
                     {
